# pi-say: what this machine's speaker needs.  Install to /etc/default/pi-say
#
# Everything here is a property of the speaker, not of whoever is speaking, so
# it lives with the machine instead of in every caller's service unit.
# Environment variables of the same names override this file; command-line
# flags override both.
#
# The values below are for a Bluetooth speaker, which is what pi5-deck has:
# a Pi 5 has no 3.5mm jack, and its 3.5" HDMI panel refuses audio outright
# (aplay: audio open error 524), so Bluetooth is the only way out.
#
#   PI_SAY_DEVICE     the A2DP sink with no ALSA conversion in front of it.
#                     Letting `plug` convert at playback time buzzes audibly,
#                     whatever rate_converter is configured.
#   PI_SAY_RATE       what the sink runs at. pi-say resamples into its cache,
#   PI_SAY_CHANNELS   so playback is a straight copy and nothing converts.
#   PI_SAY_LEAD_MS    A2DP lets the amp sleep between streams and it takes
#                     about a second to wake, so without a silent lead-in the
#                     speaker eats the start of every phrase. Digital silence
#                     was enough here; a speaker that needs real sound wants a
#                     quiet tone instead (see pi-say-poi's leading beep).
#
# A wired sink wants none of it: set PI_SAY_DEVICE to its ALSA name and leave
# the rest empty.
PI_SAY_DEVICE=btspk_raw
PI_SAY_RATE=48000
PI_SAY_CHANNELS=2
PI_SAY_LEAD_MS=1800
